,2018,2019,2020,2021,2022,2023,2024,2025],"counts":[0,0,0,1,1,2,1,1,0,0,1,3,2,1,2,0,3,1,2,3,2,4,0],"sen_per_decade":1.0,"p":0.0145,"early":{"label":"2003–2012","mean_per_year":0.6,"years":[2003,2012]},"late":{"label":"2016–2025","mean_per_year":1.8,"years":[2016,2025]},"q":0.0615,"passes_fdr":false,"display":{"subtitle":"Months per year that were more humid than 9 in 10 months of 2003–2022.","trend_text":"The change in this count could still be chance."}},"spatial":{"share_opposite_significant":0.0,"share_same_significant":0.0,"warn":false},"neighbour":null,"vs_world":null,"insights":[{"kind":"total_change","text":"Since 2003 water vapour in the air got about 7% higher.","detail":"Total change +1.52 kg/m² (95% range +0.65 to +2.43); as a share of the average: 95% range +2% to +11%.","evidence_level":"strong","tested":false,"methods_anchor":"trend-test","numbers":{"total":1.522}},{"kind":"signal_noise","text":"Since about 2014 the change has been bigger than a normal year-to-year swing.","evidence_level":"descriptive","tested":false,"methods_anchor":"signal-noise","numbers":{"year":2014,"ratio_now":2.25}},{"kind":"season_unresolved","text":"Summer shows the fastest rise in water vapour, but the seasons are still too close to tell apart.","detail":"Summer: +1.53 kg/m² per decade; the other seasons −0.09 to +1.00. Their 95% ranges overlap.","evidence_level":"descriptive","tested":false,"methods_anchor":"seasons","numbers":{"fast":1.534}},{"kind":"months","text":"Most of the rise in water vapour happens in August and September.","evidence_level":"moderate","tested":true,"methods_anchor":"months","numbers":{"months":[8,9]},"q":0.0068}],"insights_audit":{"tested":17,"passed":2,"shown":1,"q":0.05,"text":"Besides the main test, we ran 17 follow-up checks (12 months, 4 seasons, extreme months). 2 held up after correcting for running many tests at once (false discovery rate q = 0.05); most of them repeat the main trend month by month. Findings marked “follow-up check” come from these checks."},"deeper_checks":null,"provenance":{"dataset":"NASA AIRS (Aqua satellite) · TotH2OVap","version":"7.0","doi":"10.5067/UBENJB9D3T2H","landing_page":"https://doi.org/10.5067/UBENJB9D3T2H","accessed":"2026-11-15","processing":["one OPeNDAP DAP4 subset per monthly granule: SurfAirTemp_A, SurfAirTemp_D, TotH2OVap_A, TotH2OVap_D (no other fields)","masked fill value -9999","country value = mean of the 2° map squares that overlap the country, weighted by the overlapping area (mainland and islands within about 1,000 km)","monthly series 2003-01 to 2025-12 (complete years)","anomalies from the calendar-month normal; Sen's slope, Mann-Kendall (Yue-Wang), OLS with effective sample size (trend_stats.py)"],"synthetic":false,"caveat":"Data: NASA AIRS infrared sounder on the Aqua satellite, 1° squares, average of the ~1:30 am and ~1:30 pm passes; a satellite retrieval, not a weather model (MERRA-2 does use AIRS measurements, so the two are not fully independent) (averaged into 2° squares; coastal squares include some sea). The trend is a straight line through 2003–2025; it shows what changed, not why. The record is 23 years long, shorter than the 30 years usually used to describe a climate. AIRS sees mostly clear and partly cloudy skies, and its global water-vapour trend is much smaller than MERRA-2's (about +0.1 vs +0.5 kg/m² per decade, 2003–2025); treat this trend as not yet confirmed."}}